{0 Getting started}

Build a Cloudflare Worker in OCaml, run it locally, change its response, and
deploy it. This guide starts outside the workero repository.

{1 Prerequisites}

- {{:https://opam.ocaml.org/doc/Install.html}opam} with an active OCaml 5.4 or
  later switch. The source package requires Dune 3.24 or later,
  [js_of_ocaml] and [wasm_of_ocaml-compiler] 6.4.1 or later and below 6.5,
  and [jsonkit] 1.1 or later and below 2. The source installation below lets
  opam resolve these dependencies.
- {{:https://git-scm.com/}Git} to clone the source.
- {{:https://nodejs.org/}Node.js} 22 or later with npm. The template installs
  Wrangler 4.151.3 and binaryen 132 with [npm install]. binaryen supplies
  [wasm-opt], which wasm_of_ocaml needs on [PATH].

There is no published [workero] package on the opam package index yet. Install
it from this repository instead. You need a Cloudflare account only for the
deployment step.

{1 1. Create a project}

From a directory outside any Dune project, clone workero, install its package
into your active opam switch, and copy its template into a sibling directory:

{@sh[
eval "$(opam env)"
git clone https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero.git workero-source
opam pin add workero ./workero-source -y
cp -R workero-source/template my-worker
cd my-worker
npm install
]}

The [opam pin add] command installs the [workero] library and CLI from the
clone. [npm install] installs Wrangler and binaryen in [node_modules]. Keep
[my-worker] outside [workero-source] because Dune selects an outer project
root when projects are nested.

The new project contains [dune-project], [src/dune], [src/main.ml],
[wrangler.toml], and [package.json]. See {{!page-template}The project template}
for each file's role.

{1 2. Run locally}

Start Wrangler from [my-worker]:

{@sh[
npx wrangler dev
]}

Wrangler runs [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js], writes the packaged Worker
to [_worker/], and starts a local server. Look for its URL in the output,
usually [http://127.0.0.1:8787]. You do not need to log in to run locally.
Leave the server running and use a second terminal for these requests:

{@sh[
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/
curl -d ping http://127.0.0.1:8787/echo
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/env
]}

The first response is [Hello from OCaml on Cloudflare Workers], the second is
[ping], and the third is [hello from vars]. The [GREETING] value comes from
[[vars]] in [wrangler.toml].

{1 3. Write a response}

Replace [src/main.ml] with this handler. It retains the existing routes and
changes the home page response:

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path (Request.url req)) with
  | GET, "/" -> Response.text "Hello from my worker\n"
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.text req)
  | GET, "/env" ->
      Response.text
        (Option.value (Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"unset" ^ "\n")
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Not_Found "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Wrangler watches [src/] and rebuilds after you save. Run
[curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/] again. The response is now
[Hello from my worker]. {!Workero.serve} registers the handler;
{{!page-runtime_apis}Runtime APIs} lists the available request, response, and
network functions. {{!page-bindings}Bindings} covers configuration and resources.

{1 4. Deploy}

Create a {{:https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up}Cloudflare account} if you
do not have one. From [my-worker], authenticate Wrangler, then deploy:

{@sh[
npx wrangler login
npx wrangler deploy
]}

The login command opens a browser for authentication. Wrangler builds and
uploads the Worker, then prints its public URL, usually
[https://workero-demo-template.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev]. Open that URL or
request it with [curl] to see [Hello from my worker]. Your first deployment
may ask you to set up a [workers.dev] subdomain. Unlike local development,
deployment needs account authentication.

Next, read {{!page-handlers}Handlers}, {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}, and
{{!page-cli}The workero CLI}.
